{"name":"Termite Beige","hex":"#ddbb66","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"termite","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colonial insect of the infraorder Isoptera, in the order Blattodea; typically known for certain white-bodied species that devour and infest wood, though other species eat different plant matter, such as grasses.","A contemptible person."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["Of a chimpanzee: to catch termites by inserting a stick or vine into their nest and waiting for them to climb up it."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/termite"},{"term":"beige","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A colour, variously defined from a pale brown, to a yellow greyish off-white.","Debeige; a kind of woollen or mixed dress goods."]},{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Having a slightly yellowish gray colour, as that of unbleached wool.","Comfortably dull and unadventurous, in a way that suggests middle-class suburbia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/beige"}],"entries":[{"title":"Termite","blocks":[{"text":"Termites are a group of eusocial insects which consume a variety of decaying plant material, generally in the form of wood, leaf litter, and soil humus. They are distinguished by their beaded (moniliform) antennae and the soft-bodied, unpigmented worker caste for which they have been commonly termed \"white ants\"; however, they are not ants but highly derived cockroaches; they are genetically closer to some cockroach groups than these are to other cockroaches. About 2,997 extant species are currently described, 2,125 of which are members of the family Termitidae."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d3/Coptotermes_formosanus_shiraki_USGov_k8204-7.jpg/330px-Coptotermes_formosanus_shiraki_USGov_k8204-7.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Termite","wikidata":"Q546583"},{"title":"Beige","blocks":[{"text":"Beige is variously described as a pale sandy fawn color, a grayish tan, a light-grayish yellowish brown, or a pale to grayish yellow. It takes its name from French, where the word originally meant natural wool that has been neither bleached nor dyed, hence also the color of natural wool."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a5/Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg/330px-Saint_Anthony_church_in_Chamboret_07.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beige","wikidata":"Q843607"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Mastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg/250px-Mastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/a/a3/Mastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a3/Mastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg/250px-Mastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The external appearance of the giant northern termite Mastotermes darwiniensis is suggestive of the close relationship between termites and other cockroaches.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMastotermes_darwiniensis.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Isoptera.jpg/500px-Isoptera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Isoptera.jpg/1280px-Isoptera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Isoptera.jpg/500px-Isoptera.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Macro image of a worker.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AIsoptera.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/TermiteAnatomy.png/500px-TermiteAnatomy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/TermiteAnatomy.png/1280px-TermiteAnatomy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/TermiteAnatomy.png/500px-TermiteAnatomy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"General anatomy of a worker termite with Imago (reproductive) and soldier visualized; note the reduction and fusion of sclerites on the thorax and more membranous body compared to other Dictyoptera. Mandible descriptive terminology on the bottom right. The fontanelle is absent in basal termites, being found only in Neoisopteran termites.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATermiteAnatomy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg/500px-ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg/1280px-ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/af/ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg/500px-ZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Diagram showing a wing, along with the clypeus and leg","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AZooKeys-148-105-g003_Parastylotermes_krishnai.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png/500px-Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png/1280px-Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png/500px-Termite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Developmental biology of ants versus termites. As opposed to ants which have a linear and irreversible development from larval instars to adult (imago), termites exhibit a more complex and often bifurcated development which allows for more flexible caste pathways. Although in most termites, caste development is restricted to closely related pathways dependent on a variety of factors such as pheromonal cues, sex and size of an individual.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATermite_vs_Ant_Developmental_biology.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Termite-by-RalfR.jpg/500px-Termite-by-RalfR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Termite-by-RalfR.jpg/1280px-Termite-by-RalfR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/61/Termite-by-RalfR.jpg/500px-Termite-by-RalfR.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A young termite nymph featuring visible wing buds. Nymphs mainly develop into alates.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ATermite-by-RalfR.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/20200403_Termite_and_shed_wings_on_interior_window_sill.png/500px-20200403_Termite_and_shed_wings_on_interior_window_sill.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/16/20200403_Termite_and_shed_wings_on_interior_window_sill.png/500px-20200403_Termite_and_shed_wings_on_interior_window_sill.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"A termite alate with shed wings from other alates on an interior window sill. Shedding of wings is associated with reproductive swarming.[98]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3A20200403_Termite_and_shed_wings_on_interior_window_sill.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Flying_Termites_after_rain.jpg/500px-Flying_Termites_after_rain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/Flying_Termites_after_rain.jpg/500px-Flying_Termites_after_rain.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Alates swarming during nuptial flight after rain","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFlying_Termites_after_rain.jpg"}],"sounds":{"termite":[{"type":"audio/wav","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/9/9c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-termite.wav?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-termite.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-termite.wav.mp3"},{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/9/9c/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-termite.wav/LL-Q1860_%28eng%29-Vealhurl-termite.wav.ogg"}],"beige":[{"type":"audio/ogg; codecs=\"vorbis\"","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/0/08/En-us-beige.ogg?utm_source=en.wiktionary.org&utm_campaign=rest&utm_content=original"},{"type":"audio/mpeg","src":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/0/08/En-us-beige.ogg/